time now for morning rounds our look at  the medical news of the week beginning  with new concerns over tattoos and  piercings a report out this week from  the American Academy of Pediatrics  provides guidance for doctors young  people and families on potential health  issues surrounding such body  modifications here to break down that  and more as CBS News contributor dr.  Tara Narula Tara give us a sense from a  medical SPECT perspective what were some  of the issues that were brought up well  body modification has been around since  2000 BC and yet we've had surprisingly  little guidance in the medical community  about how to deal with it although it's  increasing in prevalence we know about  20 to 30 percent of our youth and  adolescents report some form of tattoos  or piercing and there are issues so  first and foremost is infection so if  the skin is not cleaned prior to having  this done or after you can get skin  infections and then if the ink or  needles are contaminated you're at risk  for blood-borne infections things like  HIV hepatitis C other issues can include  inflammation or scarring pain bleeding  or hematoma formation and allergic  reactions and then one thing that  adolescents don't often think about is  long-term ramifications so in a recent  survey about 70% of those with tattoos  and piercings report that it hurt their  chances for employment really yes still  yes  they gave some guidelines to  pediatricians to address with young  patients and families with some of those  concerns so the most important thing I  think first was the message that  pediatricians need to deal with this in  a non-critical non-judgmental way  okay need to be aware of the regulations  because they vary from state to state  they should really talk to adolescents  about researching the facilities that  they have this done with in because they  want to make sure that it is reputable  that they practice sterile technique  that they keep their infection control  similar to a doctor's office so they use  new disposable gloves new sterile  needles new ink into new disposable  containers and and that's very important  for preventing infection in addition  they want to make sure the adolescents  are up to date on their immunizations  that they don't take any medications  that could compromise their immunity  educate them on what to look for in  terms of infections afterwards and when  to seek medical care teaching them  things as simple as you know oral  piercings can lead to tooth chipping  down the road  and then the fact that tattoos are  permanent so even though we have lasers  and things I can try to remove them  they're partially effective they are  costly couples of hundreds of dollars  and you require multiple sessions  typically to treat them remember you're  going to have that tattoo when you're 90  as well yes our next topic deals with a  telling survey on the over treatment of  patients more than 2000 physicians  across multiple fields took part in this  survey 46 percent had at least 10 years  of experience a major finding around 20%  of overall medical care was deemed to be  unnecessary so what were some of the top  reasons that they thought this over  treatment is going on this is such an  important issue because it not only  increased costs and cost our country  hundreds of billions of dollars but it  can lead to patient harm and so not  surprisingly at the top of this list was  fear of malpractice so doctors really  practicing defensive medicine one of the  other issues was patient requestor  pressure so many times patients feel  well the more tests I get the better  care I must be getting and so they push  the doctor to get tested on in lab work  and then the third issue really revolved  around difficulty accessing prior  medical records and that happens very  often a patient will come to your office  and say well I had two tests done  recently the doctor can't get access to  the tests and they just reorder it a  reorder something similar and that can  lead to over treatment or over  prescription of testing did the survey  address any possible solutions here well  they talked about starting training for  doctors early so in medical school  really teaching about appropriateness of  testing teaching doctors to practice  evidence-based medicine and guideline  based care and in addition really  addressing this issue again of access to  records and making it easier with data  sharing with electronic medical records  and then one nice thing a couple years  ago that came out was consumer reports  in the American Board of Internal  Medicine published online something  called choosing wisely and this is an  excellent resource for patients they can  go on over 70 specialties have been  involved and really look up different  tests like the PSA or cancer screening  test and see what is this test about do  I need it and really promote a  conversation with their doctor all right  finally diabetics can run the risk of  hypoglycemia while exercising and a  proof-of-concept study researchers at  Binghamton University in New York  demonstrated how a paper-based patch  could help diabetics accurately measure  their glucose levels immediately after  physical activity the patches sensor is  self powered and disposable making it  ideal for working out this is really  really important because you know one of  the biggest barriers for diabetics is  fear of developing hypoglycemia when it  comes to exercise but exercise is so  important for the management of diabetes  prevention of cardiovascular disease so  having something like this that's simple  that doesn't require them bringing  lancets and their glucometer you know or  more expensive cumbersome systems would  be very beneficial it looks just like a  band-aid it's it's a you know I know how  nice really amazed the advancements of  technology helping us live our daily  well dr. taryn Rula thank you very much
 

